These exercises go further than the Linear regression section of the lab. Once you’ve tried them, check your answers against the solutions.
library(tidyverse)
library(tidymodels)
library(medicaldata)
They start from the lab’s opt_clean data and its model of birth weight on group, bw_fit. The code below makes both again, so this page works on its own.
opt_clean &lt;- opt |&gt;
  mutate(
    Birth.outcome = str_trim(Birth.outcome),
    Group = factor(Group, levels = c("C", "T"),
                   labels = c("control", "treatment"))
  ) |&gt;
  filter(Birth.outcome == "Live birth") |&gt;
  select(PID, Clinic, Group, Age, BMI, Birthweight, GA.at.outcome)

bw_fit &lt;- linear_reg() |&gt;
  fit(Birthweight ~ Group, data = opt_clean)

min(), max(), and desc()
min() and max() give the smallest and largest values, and arrange(desc()) sorts from the largest to the smallest.

penguins |&gt;
  group_by(species) |&gt;
  summarize(
    lightest = min(body_mass, na.rm = TRUE),
    heaviest = max(body_mass, na.rm = TRUE)
  ) |&gt;
  arrange(desc(heaviest))

# A tibble: 3 × 3
  species   lightest heaviest
  &lt;fct&gt;        &lt;int&gt;    &lt;int&gt;
1 Gentoo        3950     6300
2 Chinstrap     2700     4800
3 Adelie        2850     4775

fct_relevel() and fct_collapse()
A factor’s first level is its reference level in a model. fct_relevel() moves a level to the front, and fct_collapse() combines several levels into one.

penguins |&gt;
  mutate(species = fct_relevel(species, "Gentoo")) |&gt;
  count(species)

# A tibble: 3 × 2
  species       n
  &lt;fct&gt;     &lt;int&gt;
1 Gentoo      124
2 Adelie      152
3 Chinstrap    68



penguins |&gt;
  mutate(island = fct_collapse(island, other = c("Dream", "Torgersen"))) |&gt;
  count(island)

# A tibble: 2 × 2
  island     n
  &lt;fct&gt;  &lt;int&gt;
1 Biscoe   168
2 other    176

A quick model
licorice_gargle comes from a randomised trial (Ruetzler et al., 2013): before surgery, patients gargled either licorice or sugar water, and were asked about sore throat afterwards. A logistic regression compares the odds of a sore throat 30 minutes after surgery. An odds ratio below 1 means fewer sore throats with licorice.

licorice &lt;- licorice_gargle |&gt;
  mutate(
    gargle = factor(treat, levels = c(0, 1), labels = c("sugar", "licorice")),
    sore_throat = factor(pacu30min_throatPain &gt; 0, levels = c(FALSE, TRUE),
                         labels = c("no", "yes"))
  ) |&gt;
  drop_na(sore_throat)

logistic_reg() |&gt;
  fit(sore_throat ~ gargle, data = licorice) |&gt;
  tidy(exponentiate = TRUE, conf.int = TRUE)

# A tibble: 2 × 7
  term           estimate std.error statistic p.value conf.low conf.high
  &lt;chr&gt;             &lt;dbl&gt;     &lt;dbl&gt;     &lt;dbl&gt;   &lt;dbl&gt;    &lt;dbl&gt;     &lt;dbl&gt;
1 (Intercept)       0.568     0.193     -2.93 0.00337    0.386     0.824
2 garglelicorice    0.408     0.305     -2.93 0.00334    0.221     0.736

